und and so  we sent proposals and you know went to  several different agencies to ask them  if we could get support for this effort  you know a group that I'm very familiar  with the USDA they declined and said  well this wasn't really their area of  expertise even though it was an  aquaculture related area and then we  went to a km g laz and a lass  foundations or groups and these are  foundations and groups that have their  mission statement to support laboratory  animal care and research and ironically  none of them would really accept the  proposal from us because they said it  had to be original research and we were  doing an educational program so at that  point you know with everybody kind of  saying no we decided well heck we're  just going to do it ourselves and so  this was a meeting that happened in  February of 2012 it was a meeting  between the faculty are the upper  administration at gadsden state  community college and some of the upper  administration at the University of  Alabama at Birmingham including the vice  president of research dr. richard maher  case and at this meeting dr. mark case  brought a check for ten thousand dollars  to get the effort started and it was  also during this meeting that both  Gadsden State and the University of  Alabama at Birmingham pledged personnel  time and pledged facilities and  resources to get this effort going and  so this was the beginning  with this effort we had a really unique  challenge we needed to design an  entirely new curriculum based on the  needs for the animal care staff and  based on the needs of the zebrafish you  know researchers the different  researchers that would be using this  model and so we had a lot of unique  needs that we needed to address I mean  first we had to find out what those  needs were we also needed to make this  content available to a very large and  very diverse audience at both the  national and potentially an  international level we also knew that if  we were going to be truly effective we  had to be able to combine just the  theory and the content with actual  hands-on experiences excuse me and last  but not least you know this kind of  aquaculture is not something that we're  really familiar with as an aquaculture  group because it's really done a very  kind of unique systems that you know  that are provided by several different  vendors and you know have some you know  kind of unique qualities to them so the  movie we would need a lot of help with  this effort so the next step was really  reconnaissance we had to find out what  these people needed and so we started  with a preliminary survey that went out  to the zebrafish listserv and this kind  of gave us an idea about of what we were  going to be up against and then in 2011  I was invited to the zebrafish husbandry  Association's annual excuse me annual  workshop that was in New Orleans  Louisiana and at this workshop we  redistributed a pretty pretty good  survey a pretty detailed survey of what  the workforce needed and wanted the  results of that survey when they came  back and we kind of guard over  everything we found that really that  there was a huge need for qualified  technicians not only all over the  country but actually all over the world  and that there were very specific  knowledge and skills that they needed  that  they wanted from their technicians we  found out that a lot of these jobs paid  very well and almost all of them had  very generous benefits packages and we  also found out from the group that they  wanted a combination of online training  and hands-on workshops and so this  really gave us a good outline for what  we needed to do the next step was to  recruit our team and so the next year at  the zebrafish husbandry association's  annual workshop in Las Vegas Nevada I  was set with the task of putting a team  together to lead this effort I basically  chased down and pestered the heck out of  every expert I could find it got so bad  that people would 